{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794518385,"nonce":"IcRnpqTkbAYypyGGBSvn8ROZKN8=","title":"A hover change re-ran the CSS state pass over every node, and hover did not follow content that moved","message":"Two faults on one path.\n\n1. Cost. apply_stylesheet_state_styles, on a change of the hovered, pressed or focused set, matched every state rule against every node (apply_complex_selector_styles), listed every render node for SVG tag rules, and its callers then laid out the whole tree for \"a style was applied\" (text measure sync and visual bounds over every node). The hit test culled every child of a culling scroll by testing each. A hover also moved the revision of the render nodes, so the child index and the plan of native groups were made again. With a theme-sized rule set (120 hover and focus rules) over a flat list, a hover change cost 94 ms at 20,000 rows (dev profile), about 470 ms extrapolated to 100,000, plus the same again in relayout and hit test (410 ms measured at 20,000 from pointer move to the end of its frame).\n\n2. Staleness. The pointer move stops early when the cursor is still inside the bounds of the last leaf it hit. Those bounds are where the leaf was, so after a scroll or a relayout a resting pointer was \"still inside\" a row that had left: the row that scrolled under the pointer never got hover, its :hover style, its enter event or the cursor, until the pointer left the old row's rectangle. A golden of a combobox list scrolled under the pointer had recorded the wrong row highlighted.\n\nDone:\n- State rule candidates: a rule with a state is matched against the nodes reachable from the nodes holding the state, through the combinators after it, each step kept only if it matches the next compound. A unit test checks the candidates hold every match over 14 rule shapes and every possible holder.\n- render_nodes.get_mut returns a guard that moves the revision only when what derived data reads of a node changed (renderer/structure.rs lists it, with a test per kind of change), so a fill or border colour change leaves the child index and plan alone. LayoutTree::set_style leaves the epoch for an unchanged style. Text measure sync runs when a text node's font changed. layout_due_since decides whether a pass that restyled needs a layout; visual and layout animations keep it on (cn_disclosure depends on that).\n- Hit test through the child index (hit_candidates, foreground children kept).\n- At the top of a frame, when a scroll offset or the layout changed, the window and the harness run the pointer move again at the pointer after dropping the last-hit cache (EventRouter::forget_last_hit, RenderTree::scroll_signature).\n\nMeasured (dev profile, flat list, 120 extra state rules): pointer move plus its frame 410 ms -\u003e 0.06 ms at 20,000 rows, independent of the list; the state pass alone 94 ms -\u003e 0.03 ms.\n\nNot done, and why:\n- A rule such as .list:hover .row restyles every row while the list is hovered, on each pass: that is what it says, and the pass is O(rows it matches). Making it incremental needs the pass to restyle only nodes reachable from a holder whose state changed, and to leave the others as they were.\n- The first loop of the pass (elements with an id and state styles) lists every registered id; fine until rows carry ids.\n- The synthetic move is not throttled: it runs once per frame in which a scroll offset or the layout changed and hover reacts to something. Not measured in a real window with a real pointer (it cannot be done without driving the mouse); covered by hover_follows_content in the harness.\n- The golden cn_combobox_long_list_scrolled was re-recorded on this machine (it is per-machine and gitignored).","files":null}]}